# deploy-check-ci gate — enforcement sentinel (#1485)
#
# This committed marker governs whether the required-subset CI gate
# (`deploy.sh --check-required-subset`, the enumerated load-bearing check subset —
# today Check 38 hook-registry-index-freshness, Check 73
# bundle-metrics-gate-integrity, Check 77 mirror-pair-parity and Check 78
# rules-budget) BLOCKS or merely
# REPORTS when an in-scope check
# FAILs. The roster is declared ONCE, in the `rs_checks` array in
# core/deploy/deploy.sh; the enumeration above restates it and has drifted behind
# it before — the release that admitted Check 77 swept this file, the one that
# admitted Check 78 did not. The runner reads this file directly, so the sentinel is load-bearing on
# its own; the thin CI workflow (deploy-check-ci.yml) additionally consumes the
# runner's exit code. One token governs ALL members: flipping it arms every check
# on the roster at once, so a new member joins at whatever posture this token
# already carries.
#
# SCOPE BOUNDARY — this token does NOT govern the whole of Check 73. Check 73's
# discrimination fixtures also run as an enumerated step in install-tests.yml,
# which carries no continue-on-error and no paths: filter, so a discrimination
# failure is a red check-run REGARDLESS of the token below. Reading `warn` here as
# "nothing about G3-14/G3-15 can block a merge" is wrong.
#
# The first non-comment, non-blank line is the mode token:
#   warn      → report only (exit-0); an in-scope FAIL is surfaced but does not block.
#   enforce   → block on an in-scope FAIL (exit-1 propagates to the CI caller).
#
# GRADUATED. The token below shipped DORMANT (warn) for the calibration window and was
# flipped to `enforce` on the operator's gate-rollout graduation decision; its
# specificity reading and owner are recorded in the `Pre-merge load-bearing check
# subset` row of core/standards/gate-efficacy-standard.md § Declared-required contexts.
# It moves together with GATE_ROLLOUT_PHASE in core/deploy/deploy.sh, in one commit:
# nothing compares the two, so an edit to one alone leaves them out of step.
# The `Pre-merge load-bearing check subset` context is registered in branch protection
# after the flip, never before it: a context registered while this token reads `warn`
# is a required check that cannot turn red. A RETREAT reverses this flip: un-register
# the context first, then revert the flip commit's edits to this token,
# GATE_ROLLOUT_PHASE, the workflow header and the standard's flip record together, and
# past Check 74's escalation window re-date GATE_ROLLOUT_ARMED, because below `enforce`
# that deadline re-arms.
# An absent file, or a first token other than `enforce`, reads as `warn`.
#
# (Token-flagged rather than presence-flagged so the file could ship while the gate
#  stayed non-blocking — the docs reference it; mirrors close-completeness.enforce.)
enforce
